'en_content'=><<<'HTML'
<p>Opening a pack of <strong>vacuum-packed meat</strong> can release a stronger smell than expected. That does not automatically mean the meat is spoiled, but it is equally unwise to assume that every unpleasant smell is “just the vacuum”. The useful questions are <strong>what the odour is like, whether it fades quickly and what other signs the meat shows</strong>.</p>
<p>Vacuum packaging removes most of the air around the product, slowing oxidation and extending its commercial life. While the meat remains sealed, juices and volatile compounds can collect inside a very small space. When the bag is cut open those aromas are released at once. In some products the first smell falls noticeably after a few minutes in air; a foul odour that persists is different.</p>
<h2>Why vacuum-packed meat can smell stronger when first opened</h2>
<p>There is very little oxygen inside the pack and the meat may spend days in contact with its own purge. That low-oxygen environment affects colour and the way some aromas are perceived. Opening the bag suddenly releases volatile compounds that had been concentrated inside.</p>
<p>It therefore makes sense to distinguish an <strong>initial package odour from the smell that remains after brief exposure to air</strong>. There is no need to leave meat on the counter for hours. A few minutes while preparing it is enough to see whether the first aroma clearly decreases.</p>
<h2>What can be temporary and what is concerning?</h2>
<p>A closed, metallic, mildly acidic or simply unusually intense aroma may fade quickly as the surface meets oxygen. What matters is that it develops towards the normal smell of fresh meat and that no other warning signs are present.</p>
<p>A <strong>putrid, strongly sour, faecal, sickly-sweet or persistently nauseating smell</strong> is different. Be especially cautious if the pouch was swollen, had already lost its vacuum, or if the surface is slimy as well as foul-smelling.</p>
<h2>Dark or purple colour is a separate issue</h2>
<p>Beef kept in a low-oxygen package can look purple or very dark. Once exposed to air, myoglobin changes state and the surface may become brighter red. This normal colour behaviour is covered in our guide to <a href="/en/vacuum-packed-meat-purple-dark-why-colour-changes-after-opening/">why vacuum-packed meat can look purple</a>.</p>
<p>Colour alone cannot determine freshness, and neither can one first sniff. Consider the date, storage temperature, package integrity, texture, appearance and the smell after opening together.</p>
<h2>What to check before cooking</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Package integrity:</strong> the vacuum should have remained intact, without leaks or unexplained swelling.</li>
<li><strong>Date:</strong> follow the labelled date and the producer's storage instructions.</li>
<li><strong>Cold chain:</strong> meat held too warm for too long is not made safe simply by cooking it harder.</li>
<li><strong>Surface:</strong> some moisture is normal; abnormal slime together with bad odour is not.</li>
<li><strong>Smell after a few minutes:</strong> it should clearly diminish if it was mainly a concentrated package aroma.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Should you wash meat to remove the smell?</h2>
<p>No. Rinsing raw meat under the tap is not a useful way to “clean” it and can spread microorganisms in splashes around the sink and worktop. If meat needs washing to make it acceptable, water has not solved the underlying issue.</p>
<p>Open the pack, discard the purge, pat the surface dry with kitchen paper if your recipe benefits from it, then discard the paper and clean hands, knives and surfaces that touched raw meat.</p>
<h2>The liquid in the pouch is not simply blood</h2>
<p>Vacuum packs often contain visible red liquid. It is largely water released from tissue with proteins such as myoglobin. Our article on <a href="/en/red-liquid-in-meat-is-not-blood-what-it-really-is/">what the red liquid in meat really is</a> explains why it appears.</p>
<p>The amount of purge can vary with the cut, storage time, previous freezing and the muscle's ability to hold water. Liquid alone is not proof of spoilage.</p>
<h2>How long should it air?</h2>
<p>There is no magic number. The point is not to “cure” meat in the open air but to see whether a concentrated package aroma fades quickly. That change is normally apparent within a few minutes. Leaving raw meat at room temperature for long periods adds no safety benefit.</p>
<p>If the bad smell remains obvious, do not taste the meat to see whether it is acceptable. Taste is not a food-safety test.</p>
<h2>When to discard or contact the seller</h2>
<p>Stop if you find a clearly foul persistent odour, abnormal slime, swelling, loss of vacuum together with other deterioration, unusual colour plus bad smell, or a credible break in the cold chain. For a recent purchase, photograph the package, label and batch information before disposing of it and contact the seller or producer.</p>
<h2>The practical rule</h2>
<p><strong>A strong smell in the first second after opening is not automatically spoilage; an unpleasant smell that refuses to clear deserves caution.</strong> Vacuum packaging explains some aroma and colour changes, but it should never be used to dismiss a consistent set of spoilage signs.</p>
